#{[17592186363179 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363179 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363179 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "52b9287f-df57-59c5-b415-72f23606c6be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363180 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363180 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363180 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0c35c334-f400-5dc1-bf8d-276f2e3f2b88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363181 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363181 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363181 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a3c82834-ccb7-5845-b945-d22abbba57eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364125 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364125 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37936" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364125 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364125 #edb/ref 1071 "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{3}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364125 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bd6e26e7-9607-534b-af98-be903768ffa2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364125 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b],$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & 12\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Hence ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=-9\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-3-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-1-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1-{x}_{3} \\\\ -3-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2$, we have\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $T(x)=b.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=-3-(-5)=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364126 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364126 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364126 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364126 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364126 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364126 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364126 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364126 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186364128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364126 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "59e5ee21-2892-5089-bfdc-97a15df07f35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364127 #edb/ref 1080 "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364127 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364127 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03e68c41-7d11-5667-be88-8b3f622f7f5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364128 #edb/ref 1080 