92186045417] [17592186421454 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421454 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186421432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421454 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d7a56c57-7ab7-579a-a5d2-335e803bfe6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421458 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421458 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40209" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421458 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421458 #edb/ref 1071 "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{3}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421458 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dca0d5db-2f8d-5d5d-b6ae-e11ba42097a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421458 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b],$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -4 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{2}{7}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free 